-- | The NTT manager: one contract per deployment, carrying the deployment's
-- configuration and the three value-moving choices — 'Transfer' (outbound
-- send), 'Release' (inbound unlock) and 'Mint' (inbound mint).
--
-- The manager drives the CIP-56 token-standard interfaces ('Holding',
-- 'TIV1.TransferFactory', 'BurnMintFactory') directly, so any conforming token
-- works without a per-token wrapper contract.
--
-- Three parties sign every manager, and each signature has one job:
--
--   * @admin@ is the operational role: it maintains the peer table and
--     rotates the committed factory. It is transferable ('TransferAdmin');
--     transferring it to gg is the guardian quorum's custody opt-in.
--   * @guardianGovernance@ (gg), the guardians' k-of-n threshold party, is the
--     mint authority for gg-administered burn/mint instruments, the owner of
--     the deployment's transceiver 'Emitter', the replay-scope consumer, and
--     the quorum end-state of 'TransferAdmin': a gg-adminned deployment's
--     admin operations are governance actions.
--   * @nttCustody@, a second quorum party, owns the lock/unlock reserve of
--     each gg-adminned deployment. gg never owns a reserve: the reserve owner
--     is the CIP-56 transfer sender, and the sender's authority runs in
--     registry code (REDTEAM.md F1).
--
-- @reserveCustodian@ owns the lock/unlock reserve: @admin@ while @admin@ is
-- not gg, @nttCustody@ while it is ('reserveCustodianFor').
--
-- gg is the only minter. A burn/mint deployment bridges a gg-administered
-- instrument through the canonical 'Token.CIP0056.CoinFactory.CoinFactory'
-- ('requireCanonicalFactory'). External minters are not supported: NTT cannot
-- verify that a foreign factory's burn happened.
--
-- The deployment's stable identity is no longer a co-signing party — it is a
-- derived @namespace : Text@ (see 'nttNamespaceFor'), committed at
-- registration from the registering admin, the bridged instrument, and the
-- manager id. It sub-scopes gg's replay trie ('VerifyAndConsumeVAA' runs with
-- @consumer = guardianGovernance@, @namespace = mgr.namespace@) and never
-- changes, but — unlike a party — needs no ceremony to mint or safeguard: gg
-- is already a lifetime signatory of every manager, so the derived text rides
-- along on gg's inherited authority. The manager and transceiver addresses are
-- likewise bound to the registering admin at creation time and stored, so
-- they too survive 'TransferAdmin' untouched (see 'nttManagerAddressFor').
--
-- gg and @nttCustody@ sign actively only once, at the ceremony that creates
-- the 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance.NttGovernance' root; every manager inherits
-- both signatures from that root, including for the transceiver 'Emitter' minted
-- atomically inside 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance.RegisterManager'. Value can only
-- move inside the manager's fixed choice bodies, and both inbound choices
-- verify and consume a VAA first, so no signatory can move bridged value
-- alone, and relaying stays permissionless: any executor may submit, but only
-- the VAA-bound transfer can happen.
--
-- Lock/unlock custody is owned by @reserveCustodian@. A deployment registers
-- fully operational and explicitly custodial — the admin owns the reserve and
-- its users trust it, like a custodial bridge. The guardian quorum can later
-- opt in via 'TransferAdmin' to gg, which moves the reserve to @nttCustody@;
-- every inbound movement is then gated only by VAA verification. Isolation
-- between deployments follows from ownership: reserves of distinct custodians
-- cannot mix, and the @nttCustody@ pool is shared only among deployments the
-- quorum accepted. Ownership survives any registry-side reorganization of
-- holdings, which is why the partition is by owner and not by holding
-- topology. Each lock/unlock deployment also keeps a 'LockedLedger' and can
-- never release more than it locked. Burn/mint deployments are isolated by
-- instrument: the instrument id is hash-bound to the deployment namespace
-- ('nttInstrumentIdFor'), so no second deployment can register against an
-- instrument that is already bridged.
--
-- The ledger cap is wire-level accounting; the reserve can fall below it.
-- Lock merges and consolidations pay registry sender fees from the reserve;
-- 'Release' aborts on them ('SenderOutflowEqualsAmount'). Amulet's DSO can
-- archive a holding once its accrued holding fee reaches its amount. The
-- custodian tops up or refreshes the pot with 'ConsolidateCustody', and
-- 'TransferAdmin' rejects a reserve that no longer covers the balance.
--
-- Because every choice body carries all of the manager's signatures, custody
-- paths must never rely on ambient authority: 'sumCustodyHoldings' checks
-- that each custody holding the manager spends is owned by the deployment's
-- own @reserveCustodian@, so one deployment's choices can never spend another
-- custodian's holdings even when that party's authority is in scope.
--
-- The registry factory is deployment config, not per-call input. The token's
-- factory is a stateless service contract whose cid is stable (its choices are
-- nonconsuming), so the manager commits it at registration and uses that cid
-- for every operation. A caller therefore cannot route a lock, burn, unlock,
-- or mint through a look-alike factory to fake a token movement. The
-- commitment is typed by mode ('NttFactory'), matched against 'NttTokenConfig'
-- at registration and rotation, so no call site coerces a contract id. @admin@
-- refreshes it with 'SetFactory' on the rare occasion the registry is
-- upgraded; for burn/mint gg co-signs that rotation and the new factory must
-- be the canonical 'CoinFactory', for the deployment's whole life.
module Wormhole.Ntt.Manager where

import DA.Map (Map)
import DA.List (unique)
import DA.Map qualified as Map
import DA.Optional (fromSomeNote)
import DA.Date (Month (Jan), date)
import DA.Text qualified as T
import DA.Time (convertRelTimeToMicroseconds, subTime, time)

import Splice.Api.Token.AllocationV1 (Allocation)
import Splice.Api.Token.HoldingV1 (Holding, InstrumentId (..))
import Splice.Api.Token.MetadataV1 (ExtraArgs (..))
import Splice.Api.Token.BurnMintV1
  (BurnMintFactory, BurnMintFactory_BurnMint (..), BurnMintOutput (..))
import Splice.Api.Token.TransferInstructionV1 qualified as TIV1
import Token.CIP0056.CoinFactory (CoinFactory)
import Wormhole.Core.Bytes
import Wormhole.Core.Replay (ReplayNode)
import Wormhole.Core.State
  ( Emitter, CoreState, WormholeMessage
  , GetGuardianGovernance (..), VerifyAndConsumeVAA (..), PublishMessage (..) )
import Wormhole.Core.VAA (VAA)
import Wormhole.Ntt.Amount
import Wormhole.Ntt.Bytes
import Wormhole.Ntt.Cip56
import Wormhole.Ntt.Deposit
import Wormhole.Ntt.Ledger
import Wormhole.Ntt.Payload

-- | How the deployment holds bridged value: custody it (lock on send, unlock
-- on receive) or destroy and recreate it (burn on send, mint on receive). One
-- mode per deployment, fixed at registration.
data NttTokenConfig
  = LockUnlock
  | BurnMint
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The deployment's committed registry factory, typed by mode, so a
-- lock/unlock deployment cannot even hold a burn/mint factory cid (or vice
-- versa) and no call site needs an unchecked contract-id coercion.
data NttFactory
  = TransferFactoryCid (ContractId TIV1.TransferFactory)
  | BurnMintFactoryCid (ContractId BurnMintFactory)
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | Does the committed factory's type match the deployment mode? Checked at
-- registration and on every rotation, so the mismatch aborts at config time
-- rather than at the first transfer.
factoryMatchesConfig : NttTokenConfig -> NttFactory -> Bool
factoryMatchesConfig config factory = case (config, factory) of
  (LockUnlock, TransferFactoryCid _) -> True
  (BurnMint, BurnMintFactoryCid _) -> True
  _ -> False

-- | The committed transfer factory; aborts if the deployment holds a burn/mint
-- factory (impossible for a registered manager, which checks the mode match).
requireTransferFactory : NttFactory -> Update (ContractId TIV1.TransferFactory)
requireTransferFactory = \case
  TransferFactoryCid cid -> pure cid
  BurnMintFactoryCid _ -> abort "ntt: deployment's committed factory is not a transfer factory"

-- | The committed burn/mint factory; aborts if the deployment holds a transfer
-- factory (impossible for a registered manager, which checks the mode match).
requireBurnMintFactory : NttFactory -> Update (ContractId BurnMintFactory)
requireBurnMintFactory = \case
  BurnMintFactoryCid cid -> pure cid
  TransferFactoryCid _ -> abort "ntt: deployment's committed factory is not a burn/mint factory"

-- | Pin a caller-supplied 'NttFactory' to the canonical, gg-administered
-- 'Token.CIP0056.CoinFactory.CoinFactory' — the only burn/mint factory any
-- path may commit. NTT cannot verify another factory's burn, and a VAA cannot
-- name a contract id. Checks the cid is genuinely backed by the 'CoinFactory'
-- template (not merely an interface implementer) and that its @admin@ matches
-- this deployment's guardianGovernance.
requireCanonicalFactory : Party -> NttFactory -> Update ()
requireCanonicalFactory guardianGovernance factory = do
  bmCid <- requireBurnMintFactory factory
  r <- fetchFromInterface @CoinFactory bmCid
  case r of
    None -> abort "ntt governance: factory is not the canonical CoinFactory"
    -- admin is the factory's sole signatory, so this equality also proves gg
    -- authorized the factory's creation.
    Some (_, cf) -> assertMsg "ntt governance: factory's admin does not match this deployment's guardianGovernance"
      (cf.admin == guardianGovernance)

-- | Pin a caller-supplied 'CoreState' to the deployment's committed trust
-- anchor through 'GetGuardianGovernance'. @reader@ is the controller of that
-- read. guardianGovernance is a CoreState signatory, so the equality also
-- proves gg authorized the contract's creation.
pinCommittedCoreState : Party -> Party -> ContractId CoreState -> Update ()
pinCommittedCoreState reader guardianGovernance coreStateCid = do
  committedGuardianGovernance <- exercise coreStateCid GetGuardianGovernance with reader
  assertMsg "ntt: core state is not the committed guardian trust anchor"
    (committedGuardianGovernance == guardianGovernance)

-- | 'pinCommittedCoreState' with gg as the reader, then fetch the contract.
-- The pin runs before the fetch: a 'CoreState' of another gg fails the pin,
-- not the fetch authorization.
requireCommittedCoreState : Party -> ContractId CoreState -> Update CoreState
requireCommittedCoreState guardianGovernance coreStateCid = do
  pinCommittedCoreState guardianGovernance guardianGovernance coreStateCid
  cs <- fetch coreStateCid
  assertMsg "ntt: fetched core state is not the committed guardian trust anchor"
    (cs.guardianGovernance == guardianGovernance)
  pure cs

-- | A peer NTT deployment on another chain: its manager address (the
-- @recipientManager@/@sourceManager@ in the transceiver message), its
-- transceiver address (the emitter of inbound VAAs), and the
-- number of decimals of the peer's token. The decimals let an outbound
-- transfer trim to the precision the peer can represent, so no dust is silently
-- dropped when the far side rescales; this mirrors the peer @decimals@ the
-- reference @setPeer@ stores.
data Peer = Peer with
    managerAddress     : NttManagerAddress
    transceiverAddress : EmitterAddress
    decimals           : Int
  deriving (Eq, Show)

checkPeerAddressesAreWellFormed : Peer -> Bool
checkPeerAddressesAreWellFormed peer =
  checkHexValueIsWellFormed peer.managerAddress && checkHexValueIsWellFormed peer.transceiverAddress

-- | Choice-entry re-check of a VAA argument from the Ledger API.
checkVaaArgumentIsWellFormed : EncodedVaa -> Update ()
checkVaaArgumentIsWellFormed vaaBytes =
  assertMsg "ntt: vaaBytes is not a well-formed EncodedVaa" (checkHexValueIsWellFormed vaaBytes)

-- | The origin rule of the core's governance VAAs: the current guardian set
-- signed it, and the core's governance emitter emitted it.
checkVaaIsCurrentSetGovernance : CoreState -> VAA -> Update ()
checkVaaIsCurrentSetGovernance cs vaa = do
  assertMsg "ntt governance: not signed by current guardian set"
    (vaa.guardianSetIndex == cs.guardianSetIndex)
  assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong emitter chain"
    (vaa.emitterChain == cs.governanceChainId)
  assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong emitter address"
    (vaa.emitterAddress == cs.governanceContract)

isUint8 : Int -> Bool
isUint8 wireField = wireField >= 0 && wireField <= 255

isUint32 : Int -> Bool
isUint32 wireField = wireField >= 0 && wireField <= 4294967295

-- | Domain-separation tag for NTT manager addresses.
nttManagerAddressTag : Text
nttManagerAddressTag = "wormhole:ntt-manager:v1"

-- | The 32-byte NTT manager address of the 'NttManager' allocated at
-- @managerId@ for @(guardianGovernance, admin)@, where @admin@ is the REGISTERING
-- admin. Bound to the registering admin at creation, so it survives
-- 'TransferAdmin' — the address is computed once and stored, not recomputed
-- against the current (transferable) admin.
nttManagerAddressFor : Party -> Party -> Int -> NttManagerAddress
nttManagerAddressFor guardianGovernance admin managerId =
  parseHexBytesAsOrAbort (derivedAddress (DomainTag nttManagerAddressTag) guardianGovernance admin (RegistryId managerId))

-- | Domain-separation tag for the 32-byte address of a Canton party. One tag
-- for every role the party plays on the wire (recipient and sender).
partyAddressTag : Text
partyAddressTag = "wormhole:v1"

-- | @keccak256(tag ‖ lp(utf8(partyText)))@. Split from 'partyAddressFor' so
-- tests can pin the encoding against a fixed string.
partyAddressFromText : Text -> UniversalAddress
partyAddressFromText partyText =
  parseHexBytesAsOrAbort (keccak256Bytes (utf8Bytes partyAddressTag
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (utf8Bytes partyText))))

-- | The 32-byte address of a Canton party on the wire. As a recipient: the
-- value a sender computes off-chain for an outbound transfer's
-- @recipientAddress@ to reach @party@ on Canton; the inbound path recomputes
-- it from the caller-supplied recipient and checks it against the VAA. As a
-- sender: the @sender@ of the 'NttManagerMessage' that 'Transfer' publishes.
partyAddressFor : Party -> UniversalAddress
partyAddressFor party = partyAddressFromText (partyToText party)

-- | Domain-separation tag for burn/mint instrument-id bindings.
nttInstrumentIdTag : Text
nttInstrumentIdTag = "wormhole:ntt-instrument:v1"

-- | Text form of 'nttInstrumentIdFor'. Split out so tests can pin the encoding
-- against a fixed string.
nttInstrumentIdFromText : Text -> Int -> Text
nttInstrumentIdFromText adminText nonce =
  "wormhole-ntt:" <> hexTextOf (keccak256Bytes (utf8Bytes nttInstrumentIdTag
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (utf8Bytes adminText))
    <> intToBytesN nonce 8))

-- | The instrument id a burn/mint deployment's instrument must carry:
-- hash-committed to the REGISTERING admin (present as a controller at
-- registration, and whose signature co-signs the created manager), with
-- @nonce@ distinguishing multiple deployments by the same admin.
--
-- This binding is what makes a burn/mint instrument exclusive to one
-- deployment. Registration is permissionless and gg administers every
-- burn/mint instrument, so without it anyone could register a second manager
-- for an instrument that is already bridged and mint it against VAAs from a
-- peer they control. A CIP-56 instrument's id is part of its identity and
-- fixed at creation, so committing the registering admin into the id at
-- creation and checking the commitment at registration ('RegisterManager')
-- means only that
-- registering admin can ever bind a manager to the instrument. It plays the
-- role of EVM NTT's @setMinter@ pointer, attached to the immutable instrument
-- identity instead of a mutable token field.
nttInstrumentIdFor : Party -> Int -> Text
nttInstrumentIdFor admin nonce = nttInstrumentIdFromText (partyToText admin) nonce

-- | Domain-separation tag for NTT governance root identities.
nttGovernanceRootIdentityTag : Text
nttGovernanceRootIdentityTag = "wormhole:ntt-governance-root:v1"

microsecondsSinceUnixEpoch : Time -> Int
microsecondsSinceUnixEpoch genesisTime =
  convertRelTimeToMicroseconds (subTime genesisTime (time (date 1970 Jan 1) 0 0 0))

-- | The identity of the 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance.NttGovernance' root that a
-- first genesis creates: @keccak256(tag ‖ lp(utf8(gg)) ‖ uint64(genesis ledger
-- time in microseconds))@. Two first geneses under one gg at the same ledger
-- time, for example in one transaction, get the same identity. One root per gg
-- is a guardian rule.
nttGovernanceRootIdentityFor : Party -> Time -> NttGovernanceRootIdentity
nttGovernanceRootIdentityFor guardianGovernance genesisTime =
  parseHexBytesAsOrAbort (keccak256Bytes (utf8Bytes nttGovernanceRootIdentityTag
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (utf8Bytes (partyToText guardianGovernance)))
    <> intToBytesN (microsecondsSinceUnixEpoch genesisTime) 8))

-- | Domain-separation tag for NTT registration-VAA bindings.
nttRegistrationBindingTag : Text
nttRegistrationBindingTag = "wormhole:ntt-registration:v2"

-- | Text form of 'nttRegistrationBindingFor', split out so tests can pin
-- the encoding against fixed strings.
nttRegistrationBindingFromText : NttGovernanceRootIdentity -> Text -> Text -> Int -> NttRegistrationBinding
nttRegistrationBindingFromText rootIdentity guardianGovernanceText adminText nonce =
  parseHexBytesAsOrAbort (keccak256Bytes (utf8Bytes nttRegistrationBindingTag
    <> hexBytesOf rootIdentity
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (utf8Bytes guardianGovernanceText))
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (utf8Bytes adminText))
    <> intToBytesN nonce 8))

-- | The 32-byte binding a guardian-signed
-- 'Wormhole.Ntt.Payload.RegisterBurnMintManager' VAA must carry in its
-- @registrationBinding@ field: hash-committed to (root identity,
-- guardianGovernance, admin, nonce), with no @nextId@ in the preimage — a
-- signed-off-chain VAA cannot predict which @NttGovernance.nextId@ will still
-- be current when relayed, so binding to it would let a stranger grief a
-- registration by racing a junk one first. The root identity binds the VAA to
-- one 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance.NttGovernance' lineage: a second root of the
-- same gg rejects it.
nttRegistrationBindingFor : NttGovernanceRootIdentity -> Party -> Party -> Int -> NttRegistrationBinding
nttRegistrationBindingFor rootIdentity guardianGovernance admin nonce =
  nttRegistrationBindingFromText rootIdentity (partyToText guardianGovernance) (partyToText admin) nonce

-- | Domain-separation tag for the derived replay namespace.
nttNamespaceTag : Text
nttNamespaceTag = "wormhole:ntt-namespace:v1"

-- | Text form of 'nttNamespaceFor'. Split out so tests can pin the encoding
-- against fixed strings — the existing vector-pinning convention
-- ('nttInstrumentIdFromText', 'partyAddressFromText').
nttNamespaceFromText : Text -> Text -> Text -> Int -> Text
nttNamespaceFromText instrumentAdminText instrumentIdText adminText managerId =
  "ntt:" <> hexTextOf (keccak256Bytes (utf8Bytes nttNamespaceTag
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (utf8Bytes instrumentAdminText))
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (utf8Bytes instrumentIdText))
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (utf8Bytes adminText))
    <> intToBytesN managerId 8))

-- | The derived replay-trie sub-scope committed on the manager at
-- registration: @"ntt:" <> keccak256(tag ‖ instrument.admin ‖ instrument.id ‖
-- registering-admin ‖ managerId)@ (68 chars, under the core's 128-char
-- bound). This is gg's replacement for the retired @namespace : Party@ —
-- gg is already a lifetime signatory of every manager, so the trie sub-scope
-- needs no party of its own to mint or safeguard. The registering admin in
-- the preimage binds the deployment identity to its creator (surviving
-- 'TransferAdmin', which never recomputes it); @managerId@ guarantees
-- exactly one namespace per deployment, closing the corner case where two
-- deployments share both instrument and registering admin.
nttNamespaceFor : InstrumentId -> Party -> Int -> Text
nttNamespaceFor inst admin managerId =
  nttNamespaceFromText (partyToText inst.admin) inst.id (partyToText admin) managerId

-- | The parties that decide a reserve's owner. Named fields, so two of them
-- cannot be swapped silently at a call site.
data ReserveCustodyParties = ReserveCustodyParties with
    guardianGovernance : Party
    nttCustody         : Party
    admin              : Party
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The owner of a lock/unlock reserve under @admin@: @nttCustody@ when the
-- quorum holds the admin role, else @admin@. gg is never the result, because
-- the reserve owner is the CIP-56 transfer sender and registry code runs
-- with its authority.
reserveCustodianFor : ReserveCustodyParties -> Party
reserveCustodianFor parties =
  if parties.admin == parties.guardianGovernance then parties.nttCustody else parties.admin

-- | The namespace fingerprint of a Canton party id @hint::fingerprint@.
-- None unless both parts are non-empty and the id has exactly one @::@.
partyNamespaceFromText : Text -> Optional Text
partyNamespaceFromText partyText = case T.splitOn "::" partyText of
  [hint, fingerprint] | hint /= "" && fingerprint /= "" -> Some fingerprint
  _ -> None

-- | Both parties have a namespace and it is the same. The root ceremony
-- requires it of @nttCustody@ and gg. Not in an @ensure@: IDE ledger party
-- ids have no namespace.
partiesShareNamespace : Party -> Party -> Bool
partiesShareNamespace firstParty secondParty =
  case (partyNamespaceFromText (partyToText firstParty), partyNamespaceFromText (partyToText secondParty)) of
    (Some firstNamespace, Some secondNamespace) -> firstNamespace == secondNamespace
    _ -> False

-- | Check that a caller-supplied 'LockedLedger' is this deployment's own, and
-- return its payload. Only 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance.RegisterManager' can
-- create a ledger (it needs the root's signatures), and it creates exactly one
-- per lock/unlock deployment, so matching the identity fields suffices. The
-- ledger is named by @managerAddress@, which is unique per deployment, so
-- (guardianGovernance, managerAddress) alone identifies it. The
-- observer-sync check is a bug-catcher, not identity: 'TransferAdmin' is the
-- only admin-changing path and updates both contracts atomically.
resolveLedger : NttManager -> ContractId LockedLedger -> Update LockedLedger
resolveLedger mgr ledgerCid = do
  ledger <- fetch ledgerCid
  assertMsg "ntt: locked ledger does not belong to this deployment"
    (ledger.guardianGovernance == mgr.guardianGovernance
      && ledger.managerAddress == mgr.managerAddress)
  assertMsg "ntt: ledger admin observer out of sync" (ledger.admin == mgr.admin)
  pure ledger

-- | Fetch and sum custody holdings, each validated as the deployment's
-- @reserveCustodian@'s own, of this deployment's instrument,
-- unlocked, and signed by the instrument admin ('validateSettledHolding').
-- Run over every custody holding the manager spends or points at: the
-- choice bodies carry all three signatures, so without this a caller could
-- steer a choice into spending another signatory's holdings on ambient
-- authority alone.
sumCustodyHoldings : NttManager -> [ContractId Holding] -> Update Decimal
sumCustodyHoldings mgr = sumSettledHoldings CustodyHolding mgr.reserveCustodian mgr.instrumentId

-- | The hint stored back on the ledger after an operation: the single
-- resulting holding, or 'None' when the registry returned zero or several
-- (then 'ConsolidateCustody' can rebuild it).
custodyHintFrom : [ContractId Holding] -> Optional (ContractId Holding)
custodyHintFrom = \case
  [cid] -> Some cid
  _ -> None

-- | The inbound VAA verification shared by 'Release' and 'Mint' — the
-- security-critical gate, run entirely against the manager's own committed
-- configuration. In order:
--
--   1. Pin the disclosed 'CoreState' to the deployment's committed guardian
--      trust anchor (its @guardianGovernance@), the CoreState signatory whose
--      signature a forged 'CoreState' under a throwaway gg lacks.
--   2. Verify the VAA's guardian signatures and consume its digest in gg's
--      replay trie, sub-scoped to the deployment's derived @namespace@ (so
--      replay protection is unaffected by 'TransferAdmin'), atomically.
--   3. Check the VAA emitter is the configured peer transceiver for its chain,
--      and the nested message's source and recipient managers match the peer
--      and this deployment.
--   4. Bind the caller-supplied @recipient@ to the VAA's @recipientAddress@,
--      so a relayer cannot steer the payout.
--
-- Returns the decoded transfer.
verifyInboundTransfer
  :  NttManager
  -> Party                    -- ^ executor (relayer; carries no authority)
  -> ContractId CoreState
  -> ContractId ReplayNode
  -> EncodedVaa
  -> Party                    -- ^ recipient, bound against the VAA
  -> Update NativeTokenTransfer
verifyInboundTransfer mgr executor coreStateCid replayNodeCid vaaBytes recipient = do
  checkVaaArgumentIsWellFormed vaaBytes
  pinCommittedCoreState executor mgr.guardianGovernance coreStateCid
  (vaa, _node) <- exercise coreStateCid VerifyAndConsumeVAA with
    encodedVAA = vaaBytes
    consumer = mgr.guardianGovernance
    namespace = mgr.namespace
    replayNodeCid
  let peer = fromSomeNote ("ntt: no peer for source chain " <> show vaa.emitterChain)
               (Map.lookup vaa.emitterChain mgr.peers)
  assertMsg "ntt: VAA emitter is not the configured peer transceiver"
    (vaa.emitterAddress == peer.transceiverAddress)
  let wm = decodeWormholeTransceiverMessage vaa.payload
  assertMsg "ntt: wrong recipient manager" (wm.recipientManager == mgr.managerAddress)
  assertMsg "ntt: source manager is not the peer manager"
    (wm.sourceManager == peer.managerAddress)
  let mm = decodeNttManagerMessage wm.managerPayload
      ntt = decodeNativeTokenTransfer mm.payload
  assertMsg "ntt: transfer is not destined for this chain"
    (ntt.recipientChain == mgr.chainId)
  assertMsg "ntt: recipient does not match VAA recipientAddress"
    (partyAddressFor recipient == ntt.recipientAddress)
  pure ntt

template NttManager
  with
    namespace            : Text            -- derived replay-trie sub-scope under gg; nttNamespaceFor at registration
    admin                : Party           -- operational role; transferable
    guardianGovernance   : Party           -- gg: mint authority, transceiver owner, replay consumer, trust anchor
    nttCustody           : Party           -- owns the reserve while gg is admin
    reserveCustodian     : Party           -- owns the lock/unlock reserve; reserveCustodianFor of (guardianGovernance, nttCustody, admin)
    chainId              : Int             -- this deployment's own Wormhole chain id (uint16)
    managerId            : Int             -- allocated by NttGovernance; in the address preimage
    managerAddress       : NttManagerAddress  -- nttManagerAddressFor guardianGovernance <registering admin> managerId
    transceiverEmitterId : Int             -- the deployment's transceiver Emitter (owner = guardianGovernance)
    instrumentId         : InstrumentId    -- the bridged CIP-56 instrument
    tokenConfig          : NttTokenConfig
    tokenDecimals        : Int             -- the local token's decimals (0..10)
    tokenAddress         : UniversalAddress  -- nttTokenAddressFor instrumentId; stored at registration so it survives TransferAdmin
    peers                : Map Int Peer    -- chain id -> peer deployment
    factory              : NttFactory      -- the registry's committed factory, typed by mode
    paused               : Bool            -- emergency halt: blocks Transfer/Release/Mint
    factoryEpoch         : Int             -- TOCTOU pin for 'AcceptAdminTransferByVaa' and 'SetFactoryByVaa': 'SetFactory' and 'SetFactoryByVaa' bump this on every rotation, so a guardian-vetted (managerAddress, factoryEpoch) pair can never be satisfied by a look-alike factory rotated in after the quorum signed
    peerEpoch            : Int             -- freshness pin for 'SetPeerByVaa': every 'SetPeer' bumps this deployment-wide counter, so a withheld/superseded peer VAA can never reinstate a since-replaced peer
  where
    -- See the module header for what each signature contributes. All three
    -- signatures are inherited from the NttGovernance root at registration
    -- (admin from the registering caller); no signatory acts per transfer.
    -- @namespace@ is derived data, not a party. @reserveCustodian@ is always
    -- @admin@ or @nttCustody@, so its signature is already in this set.
    signatory admin, guardianGovernance, nttCustody
    ensure
      reserveCustodian == reserveCustodianFor ReserveCustodyParties with guardianGovernance, nttCustody, admin
        && reserveCustodian /= guardianGovernance
        && admin /= nttCustody
        && nttCustody /= guardianGovernance
        && checkHexValueIsWellFormed managerAddress
        && checkHexValueIsWellFormed tokenAddress
        && all checkPeerAddressesAreWellFormed (Map.values peers)

    -- | Register or replace a peer, broadcasting the registration through the transceiver. Consuming.
    -- @payer@ funds the broadcast: the admin path self-pays (@payer = admin@); 'SetPeerByVaa'
    -- passes @executor@ so the relayer self-funds and gg's fee-free invariant holds. @payer@ must
    -- co-control: Daml authorization does not carry a choice's controllers past a nested exercise
    -- on the same contract, so without this the nested 'PublishMessage' (@controller owner, payer@)
    -- would lose @executor@'s authority the moment 'SetPeerByVaa' calls in here. When @payer == admin@
    -- (the direct path) this adds nothing beyond @admin@'s own authority.
    choice SetPeer : (ContractId NttManager, WormholeMessage)
      with
        chainId               : Int
        peer                  : Peer
        transceiverEmitterCid : ContractId Emitter
        coreStateCid          : ContractId CoreState
        consistencyLevel      : Int
        payer                 : Party
        feeAllocation         : Optional (ContractId Allocation, ExtraArgs)
      controller admin, payer
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: peer chain id must be a positive uint16"
          (chainId > 0 && chainId <= 65535)
        assertMsg "ntt: peer chain id must not be this deployment's own chain"
          (chainId /= this.chainId)
        assertMsg "ntt: peer decimals must be a positive uint8"
          (peer.decimals > 0 && peer.decimals <= 255)
        assertMsg "ntt: peer manager address is not a well-formed NttManagerAddress"
          (checkHexValueIsWellFormed peer.managerAddress)
        assertMsg "ntt: peer transceiver address is not a well-formed EmitterAddress"
          (checkHexValueIsWellFormed peer.transceiverAddress)
        assertMsg "ntt: peer manager address must be non-zero"
          (not (isZeroAddress peer.managerAddress))
        assertMsg "ntt: peer transceiver address must be non-zero"
          (not (isZeroAddress peer.transceiverAddress))
        -- Uint8 on the wire; the watcher drops out-of-range messages.
        assertMsg "ntt: consistency level must be a uint8" (isUint8 consistencyLevel)
        newMgrCid <- create this with peers = Map.insert chainId peer peers, peerEpoch = peerEpoch + 1
        transceiver <- fetch transceiverEmitterCid
        assertMsg "ntt: transceiver emitter is not this deployment's"
          (transceiver.owner == guardianGovernance
            && transceiver.guardianGovernance == guardianGovernance
            && transceiver.emitterId == transceiverEmitterId)
        -- Publishes the registration for the accountant; write-once there, replacements are local-only.
        (_, msg) <- exercise transceiverEmitterCid PublishMessage with
          payload = encodeTransceiverRegistration TransceiverRegistration with
            peerChain = chainId
            peerAddress = peer.transceiverAddress
          nonce = 0
          consistencyLevel
          coreStateCid
          payer
          feeAllocation
        pure (newMgrCid, msg)

    -- | Emergency halt. While @paused@, 'Transfer', 'Release', and 'Mint' all
    -- abort; config choices ('SetPeer', 'SetFactory', 'TransferAdmin') keep
    -- working, matching the reference, where pause blocks value movement but not
    -- administration. Controlled by @admin@, so on a gg-adminned deployment
    -- pausing is a governance action. (The reference additionally allows a
    -- separate pauser role to pause but only the owner to unpause; a dedicated
    -- pauser party could be added here the same way.)
    choice SetPaused : ContractId NttManager
      with
        newPaused : Bool
      controller admin
      do create this with paused = newPaused

    -- | Repoint the committed registry factory. Needed only when the registry
    -- is upgraded and re-creates its factory under a new cid; @admin@ resolves
    -- the new cid from the registry and commits it here.
    --
    -- For burn/mint gg — the party whose mint authority the factory wields —
    -- co-signs the rotation, mirroring 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance.RegisterManager',
    -- and the new factory must be the canonical 'CoinFactory'. Without both, a
    -- mutable @admin@ could later swap in a look-alike factory that captures
    -- gg's authority. Lock/unlock's transfer factory receives
    -- @reserveCustodian@'s authority as transfer sender, so it stays
    -- @admin@-only; on a gg-adminned deployment the custodian is
    -- @nttCustody@, and this rotation is a governance action (REDTEAM.md F1).
    --
    -- Every rotation bumps @factoryEpoch@, invalidating any
    -- 'AcceptAdminTransferByVaa' VAA signed against the superseded factory.
    choice SetFactory : ContractId NttManager
      with
        newFactory : NttFactory
      controller admin :: (if tokenConfig == BurnMint then [guardianGovernance] else [])
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: factory type does not match the token config"
          (factoryMatchesConfig tokenConfig newFactory)
        case tokenConfig of
          BurnMint -> requireCanonicalFactory guardianGovernance newFactory
          LockUnlock -> pure ()
        create this with factory = newFactory, factoryEpoch = factoryEpoch + 1

    -- | Hand the operational role to @newAdmin@ and, for lock/unlock, the
    -- reserve to the 'reserveCustodianFor' @newAdmin@.
    -- Both admins authorize: the old admin resigns, and the new admin accepts
    -- what it is inheriting, naming the committed factory it vetted
    -- (@vettedFactory@ must match). In practice the handoff runs as
    -- propose-accept ('AdminTransferProposal' carries the old admin's standing
    -- consent and its 'AcceptAdminTransfer' nests this choice), so the parties
    -- need not act in one submission; exercising this directly with both
    -- authorities works too. Exactly the ledger balance moves to the new
    -- custodian; the rest of the pot stays with the old custodian, because a
    -- pot can hold other deployments' reserves ('ConsolidateCustody'). The
    -- registry must take exactly the balance from the old custodian and
    -- deliver exactly the balance to the new one. The hint points at the
    -- received holdings. The pot must cover the ledger balance: custody the
    -- hint does not track — untracked fragments, or a reserve eroded by
    -- registry fees — would stay with the old custodian, out of the
    -- deployment's reach once the role changes hands. If the coverage check
    -- fails, run 'ConsolidateCustody' first; the outgoing admin can include
    -- extra holdings of its own there to cover a shortfall. The deployment's
    -- identity — manager and transceiver addresses, the derived @namespace@
    -- replay scope, the transceiver owner (gg), the ledger — is unaffected by
    -- who currently holds @admin@, so nothing changes on the wire and no
    -- replay window opens; the ledger is recreated only to hand its admin
    -- observership to @newAdmin@ ('SetAdmin').
    --
    -- Transferring to @guardianGovernance@ is the quorum's custody opt-in: the
    -- ledger balance moves to @nttCustody@ and every admin operation ('SetPeer',
    -- 'SetFactory', a further transfer) becomes a governance action. The same
    -- choice serves admin succession between ordinary parties, and lets gg
    -- hand the role back (demotion), which moves the ledger balance from
    -- @nttCustody@ to the new admin.
    choice TransferAdmin : (ContractId NttManager, Optional (ContractId LockedLedger))
      with
        newAdmin      : Party
        ledgerCid     : Optional (ContractId LockedLedger)  -- required for lock/unlock
        vettedFactory : NttFactory
        extraArgs     : ExtraArgs
      controller admin, newAdmin
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: admin transfer to the current admin" (newAdmin /= admin)
        assertMsg "ntt: the custody party cannot hold the admin role" (newAdmin /= nttCustody)
        assertMsg "ntt: committed factory is not the one the new admin vetted"
          (factory == vettedFactory)
        let newReserveCustodian = reserveCustodianFor ReserveCustodyParties with
              guardianGovernance, nttCustody, admin = newAdmin
        assertMsg "ntt: guardianGovernance cannot own a lock/unlock reserve"
          (newReserveCustodian /= guardianGovernance)
        ledgerCid' <- case tokenConfig of
          BurnMint -> pure None
          LockUnlock -> do
            let lcid = fromSomeNote
                  "ntt: ledger cid required for a lock/unlock admin transfer" ledgerCid
            ledger <- resolveLedger this lcid
            lcid' <- case ledger.custodyHoldingCid of
              None -> do
                assertMsg "ntt: reserve not consolidated; run ConsolidateCustody before the admin transfer"
                  (ledger.balance == 0.0)
                pure lcid
              Some pot -> do
                total <- sumCustodyHoldings this [pot]
                assertMsg "ntt: custody pot does not cover the locked balance; run ConsolidateCustody first"
                  (total >= ledger.balance)
                -- The pot can hold other deployments' reserves: move only this ledger's balance.
                newCustodyHint <- if ledger.balance == 0.0
                  then pure None
                  else do
                    transferFactoryCid <- requireTransferFactory factory
                    (receivedCids, _keptByOldCustodianCids) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
                      SyncTransfer with
                        sender = reserveCustodian
                        receiver = newReserveCustodian
                        amount = ledger.balance
                        instrumentId
                      SenderOutflowAndReceiverInflowEqualAmount
                      [pot]
                      extraArgs
                    pure (custodyHintFrom receivedCids)
                exercise lcid SetCustodyHint with newCustodyHint
            -- Hand the ledger observership to the incoming admin atomically.
            Some <$> exercise lcid' SetAdmin with newAdmin
        mgrCid' <- create this with admin = newAdmin, reserveCustodian = newReserveCustodian
        pure (mgrCid', ledgerCid')

    -- | Governance-VAA-gated admin acceptance: any @executor@ permissionlessly
    -- relays a guardian-signed 'Wormhole.Ntt.Payload.AcceptAdminToGovernance'
    -- VAA against a standing 'AdminTransferProposal', completing the handoff
    -- with no live @guardianGovernance@ signature — gg's side is inherited
    -- from this manager's own signatories, the old admin's from the
    -- proposal's standing consent.
    --
    -- The proposal cid is caller-supplied and validated by its contents, not
    -- resolved by contract key: Canton 3.x key lookup can be shadowed by a
    -- decoy created in the relayer's own submission.
    --
    -- @factoryEpoch@ in the parsed action is the TOCTOU pin: it names the
    -- exact factory the guardian quorum vetted when it signed, so a hostile
    -- admin cannot rotate in a look-alike factory between vetting and this
    -- relay ('SetFactory' bumps the epoch on every rotation).
    nonconsuming choice AcceptAdminTransferByVaa : (ContractId NttManager, Optional (ContractId LockedLedger))
      with
        executor      : Party
        proposalCid   : ContractId AdminTransferProposal
        coreStateCid  : ContractId CoreState
        replayNodeCid : ContractId ReplayNode
        vaaBytes      : EncodedVaa
        ledgerCid     : Optional (ContractId LockedLedger)  -- required for lock/unlock
        extraArgs     : ExtraArgs
      controller executor
      do
        checkVaaArgumentIsWellFormed vaaBytes
        proposal <- fetch proposalCid
        assertMsg "ntt: accept-admin-by-vaa proposal's new admin is not the guardian trust anchor"
          (proposal.newAdmin == guardianGovernance)
        assertMsg "ntt: accept-admin-by-vaa proposal is not for this deployment"
          (proposal.managerAddress == managerAddress)
        assertMsg "ntt: accept-admin-by-vaa proposal is not by the deployment's current admin"
          (proposal.admin == admin)

        cs <- requireCommittedCoreState guardianGovernance coreStateCid

        (vaa, _node) <- exercise coreStateCid VerifyAndConsumeVAA with
          encodedVAA = vaaBytes
          consumer = guardianGovernance
          namespace
          replayNodeCid

        checkVaaIsCurrentSetGovernance cs vaa

        -- Exhaustive constructor dispatch: a register, rotate, or set-peer VAA
        -- must abort here, even though 'RotateToCanonicalFactory' shares this
        -- action's exact byte layout.
        case parseNttGovernance vaa.payload of
          AcceptAdminToGovernance chain targetManagerAddress targetFactoryEpoch -> do
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong target chain" (chain == chainId)
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong manager address"
              (targetManagerAddress == managerAddress)
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong factory epoch" (targetFactoryEpoch == factoryEpoch)
          SetPeerByGovernance {} -> abort "ntt governance: wrong action for AcceptAdminTransferByVaa"
          _ -> abort "ntt governance: not an accept-admin action"

        exercise proposalCid AcceptAdminTransfer with
          mgrCid = self
          ledgerCid
          vettedFactory = factory
          extraArgs

    -- | Quorum-driven peer edit for gg-adminned deployments; nests SetPeer, so it broadcasts.
    nonconsuming choice SetPeerByVaa : (ContractId NttManager, WormholeMessage)
      with
        executor              : Party
        coreStateCid          : ContractId CoreState
        replayNodeCid         : ContractId ReplayNode
        vaaBytes              : EncodedVaa
        transceiverEmitterCid : ContractId Emitter
        consistencyLevel      : Int
        feeAllocation         : Optional (ContractId Allocation, ExtraArgs)
      controller executor
      do
        checkVaaArgumentIsWellFormed vaaBytes
        cs <- requireCommittedCoreState guardianGovernance coreStateCid

        (vaa, _node) <- exercise coreStateCid VerifyAndConsumeVAA with
          encodedVAA = vaaBytes
          consumer = guardianGovernance
          namespace
          replayNodeCid

        checkVaaIsCurrentSetGovernance cs vaa

        case parseNttGovernance vaa.payload of
          SetPeerByGovernance
            { chain, managerAddress = actionManagerAddress, peerEpoch = actionPeerEpoch
            , peerChain, peerManagerAddress, peerTransceiverAddress, peerDecimals
            } -> do
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong target chain" (chain == chainId)
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong manager address"
              (actionManagerAddress == managerAddress)
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong peer epoch" (actionPeerEpoch == peerEpoch)
            assertMsg "ntt: manager is not governance-adminned" (admin == guardianGovernance)
            exercise self SetPeer with
              chainId = peerChain
              peer = Peer with
                managerAddress = peerManagerAddress
                transceiverAddress = peerTransceiverAddress
                decimals = peerDecimals
              transceiverEmitterCid
              coreStateCid
              consistencyLevel
              payer = executor
              feeAllocation
          AcceptAdminToGovernance {} -> abort "ntt governance: wrong action for SetPeerByVaa"
          RegisterBurnMintManager {} -> abort "ntt governance: wrong action for SetPeerByVaa"
          RotateToCanonicalFactory {} -> abort "ntt governance: wrong action for SetPeerByVaa"

    -- | Governance-VAA-gated factory rotation for burn/mint deployments: the
    -- guardian quorum vets a specific (chain, managerAddress, factoryEpoch)
    -- rotation off-chain, and @admin@ relays it with no live gg signature
    -- needed, mirroring 'AcceptAdminTransferByVaa'. Lock/unlock rotates via
    -- plain 'SetFactory', controlled by the current custodian.
    choice SetFactoryByVaa : ContractId NttManager
      with
        newFactory    : NttFactory
        coreStateCid  : ContractId CoreState
        replayNodeCid : ContractId ReplayNode
        vaaBytes      : EncodedVaa
        extraArgs     : ExtraArgs
      controller admin
      do
        checkVaaArgumentIsWellFormed vaaBytes
        assertMsg "ntt: SetFactoryByVaa is only for gg-minted burn/mint deployments"
          (tokenConfig == BurnMint)

        cs <- requireCommittedCoreState guardianGovernance coreStateCid

        (vaa, _node) <- exercise coreStateCid VerifyAndConsumeVAA with
          encodedVAA = vaaBytes
          consumer = guardianGovernance
          namespace
          replayNodeCid

        checkVaaIsCurrentSetGovernance cs vaa

        case parseNttGovernance vaa.payload of
          RotateToCanonicalFactory chain targetManagerAddress targetFactoryEpoch -> do
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong target chain" (chain == chainId)
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong manager address"
              (targetManagerAddress == managerAddress)
            assertMsg "ntt governance: wrong factory epoch" (targetFactoryEpoch == factoryEpoch)
          _ -> abort "ntt governance: not a rotate-to-canonical-factory action"

        requireCanonicalFactory guardianGovernance newFactory
        create this with factory = newFactory, factoryEpoch = factoryEpoch + 1

    -- | Outbound transfer. Trims the amount to the wire form, moves @user@'s
    -- tokens (lock into the custodian's reserve, crediting the deployment's
    -- 'LockedLedger', or burn, returning change), and publishes the NTT message
    -- through the deployment's transceiver 'Emitter'.
    --
    -- @user@ is the sole controller and self-pays the governance message fee
    -- (as payer it co-controls the nested 'PublishMessage', which draws the
    -- fee from @feeAllocation@). The 'Emitter's owner authority (gg) is
    -- inherited from the manager's own signatories, so 'TransferAdmin'
    -- recreating the manager without the old admin never breaks this: gg's
    -- signature is untouched regardless of who currently holds @admin@.
    -- Everything else the body exercises runs on the manager's inherited
    -- signatory authority; the CIP-56 factory checks that the input holdings
    -- belong to @user@.
    --
    -- The message id is the transceiver's next sequence number, read from the
    -- fetched 'Emitter' in the same transaction that publishes, so it equals
    -- the resulting VAA's sequence and is unique per transceiver.
    --
    -- @expectedManagerAddress@ pins the deployment the user means to send
    -- through, as 'RegisterEmitter' pins its registry with
    -- @expectedGuardianGovernance@. The user's participant typically cannot
    -- read this contract: an off-ledger service names the manager cid and
    -- discloses it. Registration is permissionless and gg signs every
    -- manager, so a look-alike deployment registered by someone else is
    -- just as genuine, with its own admin as reserve custodian. The user
    -- passes the manager address it trusts (its deployment config), and the
    -- choice aborts before any token moves or anything is published unless
    -- this manager has that address.
    nonconsuming choice Transfer : WormholeMessage
      with
        user                  : Party
        transceiverEmitterCid : ContractId Emitter
        coreStateCid          : ContractId CoreState
        ledgerCid             : Optional (ContractId LockedLedger)  -- required for lock/unlock
        recipientChain        : Int
        recipientAddress      : UniversalAddress
        rawAmount             : Int      -- integer units at tokenDecimals
        nonce                 : Int
        consistencyLevel      : Int
        inputHoldingCids      : [ContractId Holding]
        feeAllocation         : Optional (ContractId Allocation, ExtraArgs)  -- message fee; None at fee 0
        extraArgs             : ExtraArgs
        -- The transfer aborts unless this manager's managerAddress is this
        -- value (see above).
        expectedManagerAddress : NttManagerAddress
      controller user
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: deployment is paused" (not paused)
        assertMsg "ntt: manager address does not match the caller's expected manager"
          (expectedManagerAddress == managerAddress)
        -- Uint8 and uint32 on the wire; the watcher drops out-of-range messages.
        assertMsg "ntt: consistency level must be a uint8" (isUint8 consistencyLevel)
        assertMsg "ntt: nonce must be a uint32" (isUint32 nonce)
        assertMsg "ntt: recipient address is not a well-formed UniversalAddress"
          (checkHexValueIsWellFormed recipientAddress)
        assertMsg "ntt: recipient address must be non-zero"
          (not (isZeroAddress recipientAddress))
        let peer = fromSomeNote ("ntt: no peer for chain " <> show recipientChain)
                     (Map.lookup recipientChain peers)
            trimmed = trimAmountTo rawAmount tokenDecimals peer.decimals
            amount = trimmedToDecimal trimmed
        assertMsg "ntt: transfer amount is zero after trimming" (amount > 0.0)
        -- Reject dust the peer cannot represent: the trimmed amount must
        -- rescale back to exactly what the user asked to send, so the amount
        -- locked or burned here equals the amount the peer will release or
        -- mint. Without this a low-decimals peer would silently drop precision.
        assertMsg "ntt: transfer amount has dust the peer cannot represent"
          (untrimAmount trimmed tokenDecimals == rawAmount)
        transceiver <- fetch transceiverEmitterCid
        assertMsg "ntt: transceiver emitter is not this deployment's"
          (transceiver.owner == guardianGovernance
            && transceiver.guardianGovernance == guardianGovernance
            && transceiver.emitterId == transceiverEmitterId)
        case tokenConfig of
          -- Lock: move the user's holdings into the custodian's reserve, merge
          -- the fresh deposit with the existing pot so the deployment keeps a
          -- single custody holding, and credit the ledger — all atomically.
          LockUnlock -> do
            let lcid = fromSomeNote
                  "ntt: ledger cid required for a lock/unlock transfer" ledgerCid
                custodian = reserveCustodian
            transferFactoryCid <- requireTransferFactory factory
            ledger <- resolveLedger this lcid
            (depositCids, _) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
              SyncTransfer with
                sender = user
                receiver = custodian
                amount
                instrumentId
              ReceiverHoldingsSumToAmount
              inputHoldingCids
              extraArgs
            let potCids = case ledger.custodyHoldingCid of
                  Some pot -> pot :: depositCids
                  None -> depositCids
            newHint <- case potCids of
              -- Already a single holding (first lock, or an empty pot).
              [single] -> do
                _ <- sumCustodyHoldings this [single]
                pure (Some single)
              _ -> do
                total <- sumCustodyHoldings this potCids
                (mergedCids, _) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
                  SyncTransfer with
                    sender = custodian
                    receiver = custodian
                    amount = total
                    instrumentId
                  ReceiverHoldingsSumToAmount
                  potCids
                  extraArgs
                pure (custodyHintFrom mergedCids)
            _ <- exercise lcid Credit with amount, newCustodyHint = newHint
            pure ()
          -- Burn: spend the user's holdings for exactly the wire amount and
          -- return the rest as change.
          BurnMint -> do
            burnMintFactoryCid <- requireBurnMintFactory factory
            assertMsg "ntt: duplicate input holding" (unique inputHoldingCids)
            holdingAmounts <- forA inputHoldingCids \cid -> do
              hv <- view <$> fetch cid
              assertMsg "ntt: burn holding not owned by sender" (hv.owner == user)
              assertMsg "ntt: burn holding instrument mismatch" (hv.instrumentId == instrumentId)
              assertMsg "ntt: burn holding is locked" (hv.lock == None)
              pure hv.amount
            let total = sum holdingAmounts
            assertMsg "ntt: input holdings do not cover the burn amount" (total >= amount)
            let change = total - amount
                outputs =
                  if change == 0.0
                    then []
                    else [ BurnMintOutput with owner = user, amount = change, context = extraArgs.context ]
            -- The choice's controllers are the factory's view admin plus
            -- @extraActors@; the committed canonical 'CoinFactory' pins the
            -- view admin to gg, so the factory body carries exactly {gg, user}.
            _ <- exercise burnMintFactoryCid BurnMintFactory_BurnMint with
              expectedAdmin = guardianGovernance
              instrumentId
              inputHoldingCids
              outputs
              extraActors = [user]
              extraArgs
            pure ()
        let ntt = NativeTokenTransfer with
              decimals = trimmed.decimals
              amount = trimmed.amount
              sourceToken = tokenAddress
              recipientAddress
              recipientChain
              additionalPayload = mempty
            mm = NttManagerMessage with
              id = parseHexBytesAsOrAbort (intToBytesN transceiver.sequence 32)
              sender = partyAddressFor user
              payload = encodeNativeTokenTransfer ntt
            wm = WormholeTransceiverMessage with
              sourceManager = managerAddress
              recipientManager = peer.managerAddress
              managerPayload = encodeNttManagerMessage mm
              transceiverPayload = mempty
        (_, msg) <- exercise transceiverEmitterCid PublishMessage with
          payload = encodeWormholeTransceiverMessage wm
          nonce
          consistencyLevel
          coreStateCid
          payer = user
          feeAllocation
        pure msg

    -- | Inbound unlock (lock/unlock deployments), permissionless. Any
    -- @executor@ relays the VAA; 'verifyInboundTransfer' is the gate. The
    -- transfer spends the deployment's consolidated pot (the ledger's custody
    -- hint) unless the caller supplies explicit holdings; the sender change
    -- becomes the new pot. The ledger is then debited, and 'Debit' rejects
    -- anything above the balance, so this deployment can never pay out more
    -- than it locked. Returns the successor ledger cid and the decoded
    -- transfer.
    --
    -- The settled amount is checked on the custodian side
    -- ('SenderOutflowEqualsAmount'): only the recipient and the registry can
    -- fetch the recipient's new holding.
    nonconsuming choice Release : (ContractId LockedLedger, NativeTokenTransfer)
      with
        executor         : Party
        coreStateCid     : ContractId CoreState
        replayNodeCid    : ContractId ReplayNode
        vaaBytes         : EncodedVaa
        recipient        : Party
        ledgerCid        : ContractId LockedLedger
        inputHoldingCids : [ContractId Holding]      -- explicit override; [] = use the pot hint
        extraArgs        : ExtraArgs
      controller executor
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: deployment is paused" (not paused)
        assertMsg "ntt: release is only for lock/unlock deployments"
          (tokenConfig == LockUnlock)
        ledger <- resolveLedger this ledgerCid
        ntt <- verifyInboundTransfer this executor coreStateCid replayNodeCid vaaBytes recipient
        let wireAmount = TrimmedAmount with decimals = ntt.decimals, amount = ntt.amount
            releaseAmount = untrimToDecimal wireAmount tokenDecimals
        assertMsg "ntt: received amount is zero at this token's decimals"
          (releaseAmount > 0.0)
        assertMsg "ntt: release exceeds this deployment's locked balance"
          (releaseAmount <= ledger.balance)
        inputs <- case (inputHoldingCids, ledger.custodyHoldingCid) of
          ([], Some pot) -> pure [pot]
          ([], None) -> abort "ntt: no custody hint on the ledger; supply explicit holdings"
          (cids, _) -> pure cids
        -- The body carries every signatory's authority, so validate that the
        -- holdings being spent are the reserve custodian's own — a caller must
        -- not be able to steer the release into another signatory's holdings.
        _ <- sumCustodyHoldings this inputs
        transferFactoryCid <- requireTransferFactory factory
        (_, changeCids) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
          SyncTransfer with
            sender = reserveCustodian
            receiver = recipient
            amount = releaseAmount
            instrumentId
          SenderOutflowEqualsAmount
          inputs
          extraArgs
        ledgerCid' <- exercise ledgerCid Debit with
          amount = releaseAmount
          newCustodyHint = custodyHintFrom changeCids
        pure (ledgerCid', ntt)

    -- | Merge custody holdings back into a single pot and repoint the
    -- ledger's hint at it: the repair path after a registry-side
    -- reorganization, an operation that left multiple change holdings, or
    -- releases that used explicit inputs. A single supplied holding just
    -- repoints the hint, with no registry transfer; two or more are merged
    -- through the factory. This is also the custodian's top-up path: the
    -- holdings are only checked to be the custodian's own of this
    -- instrument, so the custodian can pass extra holdings to restore a
    -- reserve eroded by registry fees.
    --
    -- Permissionless, so relayer tooling can run it opportunistically. On a
    -- registry that charges the sender fees the merge itself costs the
    -- reserve money, but a stranger cannot grind it down for free: the
    -- single-holding call moves nothing, and a merge needs a second
    -- custodian-owned holding, which the stranger must donate. The holdings
    -- carry no deployment tag, so callers should pass this deployment's own
    -- fragments; merging another deployment's pot is value-neutral but
    -- stales that deployment's hint, which it can repair the same way.
    nonconsuming choice ConsolidateCustody : ContractId LockedLedger
      with
        caller             : Party
        ledgerCid          : ContractId LockedLedger
        custodyHoldingCids : [ContractId Holding]
        extraArgs          : ExtraArgs
      controller caller
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: consolidation is only for lock/unlock deployments"
          (tokenConfig == LockUnlock)
        _ <- resolveLedger this ledgerCid
        case custodyHoldingCids of
          [] -> abort "ntt: no custody holdings supplied"
          [single] -> do
            _ <- sumCustodyHoldings this [single]
            exercise ledgerCid SetCustodyHint with newCustodyHint = Some single
          _ -> do
            total <- sumCustodyHoldings this custodyHoldingCids
            let custodian = reserveCustodian
            transferFactoryCid <- requireTransferFactory factory
            (mergedCids, _) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
              SyncTransfer with
                sender = custodian
                receiver = custodian
                amount = total
                instrumentId
              ReceiverHoldingsSumToAmount
              custodyHoldingCids
              extraArgs
            exercise ledgerCid SetCustodyHint with
              newCustodyHint = custodyHintFrom mergedCids

    -- | Inbound mint (burn/mint deployments), permissionless. Any @executor@
    -- relays the VAA; 'verifyInboundTransfer' is the gate. The mint runs
    -- inside the recipient's standing 'DepositPreapproval' (checked to match
    -- the recipient and this deployment's instrument), which supplies the
    -- recipient's signature while the manager supplies gg's instrument-admin
    -- authority — so the recipient can be offline. If the recipient has not
    -- opted in, the transaction aborts before the VAA digest is consumed, and
    -- the same VAA stays deliverable later.
    nonconsuming choice Mint : NativeTokenTransfer
      with
        executor              : Party
        coreStateCid          : ContractId CoreState
        replayNodeCid         : ContractId ReplayNode
        vaaBytes              : EncodedVaa
        recipient             : Party
        depositPreapprovalCid : ContractId DepositPreapproval
        extraArgs             : ExtraArgs
      controller executor
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: deployment is paused" (not paused)
        assertMsg "ntt: mint is only for burn/mint deployments"
          (tokenConfig == BurnMint)
        ntt <- verifyInboundTransfer this executor coreStateCid replayNodeCid vaaBytes recipient
        pre <- fetch depositPreapprovalCid
        assertMsg "ntt: deposit pre-approval is not for this recipient"
          (pre.owner == recipient)
        assertMsg "ntt: deposit pre-approval is for a different instrument"
          (pre.instrumentId == instrumentId)
        let wireAmount = TrimmedAmount with decimals = ntt.decimals, amount = ntt.amount
            mintAmount = untrimToDecimal wireAmount tokenDecimals
        assertMsg "ntt: received amount is zero at this token's decimals"
          (mintAmount > 0.0)
        burnMintFactoryCid <- requireBurnMintFactory factory
        exercise depositPreapprovalCid Deposit with
          amount = mintAmount
          factoryCid = burnMintFactoryCid
          extraArgs
        pure ntt

-- | The current admin's standing offer to hand the operational role to
-- @newAdmin@: the propose half of the admin handoff. A single self-signed
-- create by the proposing admin; @newAdmin@ accepts at its own pace with
-- 'AcceptAdminTransfer', or the proposer takes the offer back with
-- 'RetractAdminTransfer'. The offer pins the deployment (@managerAddress@) and
-- the proposing admin, so a proposal goes stale — and rejects — if the role
-- has changed hands since it was made.
template AdminTransferProposal
  with
    admin          : Party    -- the proposing (current) admin
    newAdmin       : Party
    managerAddress : NttManagerAddress  -- binds the offer to one deployment
  where
    signatory admin
    observer newAdmin
    ensure checkHexValueIsWellFormed managerAddress

    -- | Accept the offer: exercises 'TransferAdmin' on the deployment's
    -- manager, with the old admin's authority supplied by this contract's
    -- stored signature and the new admin's by the controller. The acceptor
    -- names the committed factory it vetted, exactly as in the direct
    -- ceremony.
    choice AcceptAdminTransfer : (ContractId NttManager, Optional (ContractId LockedLedger))
      with
        mgrCid        : ContractId NttManager
        ledgerCid     : Optional (ContractId LockedLedger)  -- required for lock/unlock
        vettedFactory : NttFactory
        extraArgs     : ExtraArgs
      controller newAdmin
      do
        mgr <- fetch mgrCid
        assertMsg "ntt: proposal is not for this deployment"
          (mgr.managerAddress == managerAddress)
        assertMsg "ntt: proposal is not by the deployment's current admin"
          (mgr.admin == admin)
        exercise mgrCid TransferAdmin with newAdmin, ledgerCid, vettedFactory, extraArgs

    -- | The proposer takes the offer back; nothing else changes.
    choice RetractAdminTransfer : ()
      controller admin
      do pure ()
